# CODEOWNERS — ADVISORY ONLY. THIS FILE GATES NOTHING.
#
# Read every line below as "route this diff to a human", never as "this path
# is protected". `required_pull_request_reviews` is NOT configured on `main`,
# so a CODEOWNERS entry only auto-REQUESTS a review from the listed owner. It
# does not make that review mandatory and it does not stop a merge. A future
# reader who assumes otherwise will believe these paths are gated when they
# are not.
#
# Turning required reviews ON would not fix that — it would deadlock ordinary
# work on this repository. `enforce_admins` is true and @Macrophage87 is the
# sole maintainer, and GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull
# request, so every PR the maintainer authors — 18 of the 21 opened to date —
# would become unmergeable. (The handful from other accounts could still be
# approved, which is exactly the inversion to avoid: the outside PRs would
# merge and the maintainer's would not.)
#
# The only thing on `main` that actually BLOCKS a merge is the required
# `ci-required` status check. Enforcement for the agent operating prompts
# therefore lives in the `agent-prompt-guard` job in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml, which FAILS the run when an untrusted author
# modifies one of them and feeds into `ci-required`. This file is the
# visibility half of that pair; ci.yml is the enforcement half. Keep the two
# lists deliberately different — see the note on each section below.
#
# Syntax: patterns are anchored to the repository root by the leading `/`; a
# trailing `/` covers the directory recursively. Last match wins, so the most
# specific rule for a path should be the last one that matches it.

# --- Agent operating prompts -------------------------------------------------
# These files are executable policy wearing a documentation disguise: their
# CONTENT is the prompt an automated actor runs on, so editing one changes
# what that actor does. This is the ONLY section that is ALSO enforced
# (blocking) by `agent-prompt-guard` in .github/workflows/ci.yml. If you add a
# file here, decide deliberately whether it belongs in that job's PROTECTED
# list too.
#
# Note the asymmetry, and do not assume this file covers it: these patterns are
# exact and CASE-SENSITIVE, so a lowercase twin such as
# docs/code_review_agent.md matches no rule here and draws no review request —
# even though on a case-insensitive checkout it is the same file as the entry
# below. That gap is closed on the blocking side only: the guard compares
# LOWERCASED paths.
/docs/AGENT_PROMPT.md         @Macrophage87
/docs/CODE_REVIEW_AGENT.md    @Macrophage87

# --- CI definition and its tooling -------------------------------------------
# Genuinely executable: these files ARE what CI does. Both are listed here for
# visibility only and deliberately NOT in the blocking guard — but for two
# DIFFERENT reasons, and conflating them overstates the guard.
#
# .github/workflows/: a hard block buys nothing against a hostile PR, because a
# `pull_request` run executes the workflow file from the PR head, so the guard
# cannot protect itself against an edit in the same PR.
#
# scripts/: that argument does NOT apply. The guard step is pure bash + git and
# executes nothing from scripts/, so a hostile scripts/ edit in a PR that left
# ci.yml alone would in fact be catchable. It is excluded on the other reason
# alone — blocking it would red every good-faith outside contribution to CI
# plumbing. That is a consciously accepted residual, and a real one:
# scripts/check_ciq_tests.py is the sole verdict of the required run-tests
# check, so a hostile edit there could make a red suite report green.
/.github/workflows/           @Macrophage87
/scripts/                     @Macrophage87

# --- The documented CI contract ----------------------------------------------
# Descriptive, not executable — but it is the sole human-readable copy of the
# SDK container digest and the SDK-bump checklist, and NOTHING cross-checks it
# against the workflow. A wrong edit here misleads the next bump rather than
# breaking a build, which is exactly the failure a second pair of eyes catches
# and CI cannot.
/docs/CI.md                   @Macrophage87

# --- This file ---------------------------------------------------------------
